1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,509
This is Vinni running at about his mile race pace, the day after a race, so he's tired here.

2
00:00:06,509 --> 00:00:13,939
Everything is eight times slower than real life, and the lines on his body are computer tracking of his joints.

3
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:19,232
He ran between a four forty-four and a four fifty-eight mile pace.

4
00:00:19,232 --> 00:00:20,427
His form is clean.

5
00:00:20,427 --> 00:00:35,565
His shin is nearly vertical at touchdown, his touchdown distance is small, so the foot lands only a little in front of his hips, his knee bends a normal amount during ground contact, and he pushes off with a nearly straight leg.

6
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:43,857
The one number that stands out is vertical oscillation, how much his centre of mass rises and falls each step.

7
00:00:43,857 --> 00:00:46,348
The yellow dot is his centre of mass.

8
00:00:46,348 --> 00:00:50,590
At mile pace it moves up and down about four inches every step.

9
00:00:50,590 --> 00:00:54,361
In his kick, running much faster, it's only about three.

10
00:00:54,361 --> 00:00:58,670
Lower vertical oscillation usually means better running economy.

11
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:02,306
Cue: run tall and quick, push the ground back, not up.

12
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:16,211
To practise that, do mile-pace strides with a metronome set a little faster than his natural cadence, about a hundred and eighty steps per minute, plus pogo hops, small quick jumps off the ankles.

13
00:01:16,211 --> 00:01:18,598
Back off if his shins start to hurt.

14
00:01:19,483 --> 00:01:21,845
How he speeds up is already right.

15
00:01:21,845 --> 00:01:31,015
From mile pace to his kick, his step rate rises about twenty-five percent, his step length a bit, and his ground contact time drops.

16
00:01:31,015 --> 00:01:36,294
That's how top fifteen-hundred-metre runners do it, so leave the kick alone.

17
00:01:37,183 --> 00:01:38,682
And a bonus: the backflip.

18
00:01:38,682 --> 00:01:45,424
The jump is great: straight up, his centre of mass rises about fourteen inches, and he lands right where he took off.

19
00:01:45,424 --> 00:01:46,865
But watch his upper body.

20
00:01:46,865 --> 00:01:52,052
It stays stuck, leaning back, while he rises, and he only tucks his knees at the very top.

21
00:01:52,052 --> 00:01:57,584
That's too late, so he lands about a quarter turn short, bent forward with almost straight legs.

22
00:01:57,584 --> 00:02:03,866
The fix: knees to your chest right after you leave the ground, then land in a small squat with your chest up.

23
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:05,941
Practise on a mat or with a spotter.

24
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:09,362
His left and right sides look the same.

25
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:14,518
We'll film again in three to four weeks, once fresh and once tired, to compare.
